nodecode-health-data is a rewrite of Infinite-Labs-AI's Infinite-Labs-AI/hermes-health-apollo:

  https://github.com/Infinite-Labs-AI/hermes-health-apollo
  commit 9f2db670a2669c9f3401fecb3b547ed52bcb2c3b
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Ultima AI, Inc. (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the question the plugin exists to answer, and the three verbs that
answer it — health_sync, health_query and health_log_food — over a local store
of one row a day: Oura's daily sleep, readiness (with its resting-heart-rate
contributor and temperature deviation), activity (score, steps, active
calories), stress (high and recovery seconds, the day's summary) and SpO2, plus
the long sleep period (duration, efficiency, average HRV, lowest heart rate,
bedtime). health_query keeps the original's recent / date_range / stress_days /
coverage shapes, its Pearson correlation over a shifted pairing (so last
night's sleep can be compared with today's readiness), and its food log with
calories optional and counted apart when absent. The coach's answering rules —
cite dates and values, compare against the user's own baseline, frame
cross-domain links as associations, say when data is thin, never invent a
number, never diagnose — are the plugin's skills/health-coach/SKILL.md, kept as
the manual (help :health-data). The Python (requests, SQLite) is Common Lisp
here: nlk:http for Oura's v2 REST API, one section declaration for the
settings, three nlk:define-contract declarations for the tools' arguments, and
JSON lines under the organism's home for the store.

Left out: the Google Health connector and its OAuth application (accounts,
token refresh, PKCE) — an Oura personal access token needs no application of
the user's own, and a second provider doubles the setup for a cell that answers
from one; the browser OAuth flow and its loopback callback; the six-hour cron
that ran a sync launcher script; /health's connect, setup and doctor
subcommands, replaced by the section and one sync verb; the calendar peek
through a host Google Workspace helper; and the analysis-pack layer
(health_feature_query, health_analysis_plan, health_analyze,
health_analysis_explain), whose feature materialization and provenance tables
were built for that second provider. A query_type correlates two metrics
instead, which is the question the packs mostly answered.
